# Audience

User-facing docs in `docs/` are written for SDK users, not for the people who
built the platform. Assume the reader knows Python and their science domain, but
knows nothing about our internal architecture.

# Write for what the user NEEDS to run something

- Lead with what the user needs to provide (inputs), what they call, and what
  they get back. A reader should be able to run the thing from the intro alone.
- State prerequisites explicitly (e.g. must be logged in with `deeporigin login`,
  whether a call blocks, what type is returned).

# Do NOT use internal jargon

Never expose internal implementation concepts in user docs. These describe how
we built the tool, not how the user runs it. Banned unless first defined in plain
terms (usually: just delete them):

- "served tool", "direct execution", "sync-only tool", "execution DTO"
- exchange / queue / microservice names, internal service names
- backend framework or infra terms (MikroORM, Argo, RabbitMQ, etc.)

If a concept is genuinely load-bearing for the user, explain it in plain language
(e.g. say "`run()` blocks until the job finishes" instead of "direct execution").
Implementation notes belong in code docstrings or `docs/dev/`, not user docs.

# Never use an acronym before defining it

Every acronym must be spelled out on first use, with the acronym in parentheses
immediately after — e.g. "matched-molecular-pair (MMP)". After that first
definition you may use the acronym alone. This applies per document (including
tables and headings): the first occurrence anywhere in the doc must be the
defined one. When the acronym names a third-party concept, also link it (see
below).

# Link third-party proper nouns

When docs mention a third-party proper noun — a company, project, tool, library,
dataset, algorithm, or standard — link it to an authoritative source on first
mention, when a link would help the reader (e.g. to learn what it is or find its
docs). Prefer the official site, repo, or docs; a canonical reference (e.g.
Wikipedia) is fine for general concepts.

- Link once, on the first mention, not every occurrence.
- Only link when it benefits the reader; skip ubiquitous names (Python, JSON)
  where a link adds nothing.
- Do not link our own products this way — use the internal doc/API reference.

# Mark external links with an icon

Any link that leaves the documentation site (its target starts with `http://`
or `https://`) must append the `:octicons-link-external-16:` icon to the end of
the link text, with a single space before it:

- Good: `[relative binding free energy (RBFE) :octicons-link-external-16:](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free-energy_perturbation)`
- Bad: `[relative binding free energy (RBFE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free-energy_perturbation)`

This applies to every external link, including links to Deep Origin web apps
(e.g. `os.deeporigin.io`). Do NOT add the icon to relative/internal links
between docs pages (e.g. `[Docking](./docking.md)`) — those stay on the site.

Do not touch auto-generated or non-published files: `docs/changelog.md`
(generated by `auto-changelog`), `docs/adr/**`, and `docs/notebooks/clean/**`.
